IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Legal Profession Uniform Law (WA) Part 4.3 -- legal costs, other than costs agreements: who the Part protects (s 170), fair
-- and reasonable costs (s 172(4)), costs disclosure (ss 174, 177) and what follows from a failure to disclose (s 178, General
-- Rules r 72A), the client's right to a costs agreement (s 179), bills, itemised bills and progress reports (ss 187, 190, 191),
-- suing for unpaid costs (s 194), interest (s 195, General Rules r 75), costs assessment (ss 197, 198, 204), and when unfair
-- costs can be held against the lawyer (ss 188, 207).
--
-- SEAT: the lawyer (Daniel, an employee solicitor at Swan & Partners; d038, 4 March 2029: "Acts for a client in an arbitration
-- under a conditional costs agreement with a 25% uplift"). The duties fall on the "law practice"; for him they are what he and
-- his firm must tell the client, when the firm may bill, sue and charge interest, what the client can do about the bill, and when
-- an unfair bill is a conduct matter against him. The client's rights are asked as he would meet them: "Can my client still ask
-- for an itemised bill?", "Can my client have my costs assessed?".
--
-- DIVISION OF GROUND: Part 4.3 Division 4 (costs agreements: ss 180-185, conditional costs agreements, uplift fees, contingency
-- fees) and Sch 4 cl 18(1) (whether Part 4.3 governs a matter) are encoded in the Application Act baseline (subject
-- wa/legal-profession-uniform-law-application-act-2022, encoding baseline-2026-10, lpula-costs.l4) and are not repeated here.
-- Sch 4 cl 18(3), (4) (the disclosure thresholds) are used here.
--
-- Provisions covered: [Uniform Law] ss 170(1), (2) (paras (a), (b)(i)-(iii), (d)-(h)), 172(4), 174(1), (2), (4)-(8), 177, 178(1),
-- 179, 187(1)-(3), 188, 190(1)-(3), 191, 194, 195, 197, 198(1), (3), (4), (7)(b), 204(2), 207, Sch 4 cl 18(3), (4); [General
-- Rules] rr 71, 72, 72A, 75.
--
-- Edition: as in lpul-practice.l4 -- [Uniform Law] mrdoc 46913 at ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_46913.txt; [General Rules]
-- mrdoc 45732, version 00-a0-02, at ../../../legal-profession-uniform-general-rules-2015/registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_45732.txt.
-- The Reserve Bank's Cash Rate Target (General Rules r 75) is an INPUT. Which body is the "designated tribunal" and how a WA costs
-- assessment is applied for are set by the Application Act and the local regulations; they are not needed for these questions.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: third party payers (ss 171, 176, 198(5), (6), (10)); disclosure where another law practice is retained (s 175,
-- except as a fact in s 177); the fair-and-reasonable factors themselves (s 172(1)-(3), s 200: judgments for the costs assessor);
-- avoiding increased costs (s 173); how a bill is given (s 189, General Rules r 73); interim bills (s 193); the itemised bill
-- higher than the lump sum (General Rules r 74); the costs assessment procedure, reasons, referral and appeal (ss 199, 201-203,
-- 205); costs disputes before the Board (Chapter 5); security for costs (s 206); the joint-venture and controlled-subsidiary
-- limbs of "commercial or government client" (s 170(2)(b)(iv), (v), (c), (3), (4)).
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill, from the seat of the
-- individual to whom the law is applied, with the design defaults ruled on 2026-10-18. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Legal Profession Uniform Law (WA) -- legal costs`

§§ `helpers`

-- The same day of the month n months later; a day the later month lacks becomes its last day.
GIVEN `d` IS A DATE
      `n` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`months after` MEANS
    Date (IF DATE_DAY `d` GREATER THAN `last day` THEN `last day` ELSE DATE_DAY `d`)
         (DATE_MONTH `d` PLUS `n`)
         (DATE_YEAR `d`)
    WHERE
        `last day` MEANS Day (Date 1 (DATE_MONTH `d` PLUS `n` PLUS 1) (DATE_YEAR `d`))
                   MINUS Day (Date 1 (DATE_MONTH `d` PLUS `n`) (DATE_YEAR `d`))

§§ `s 170, General Rules r 71 -- is my client a commercial or government client`

-- [Uniform Law] s 170(1): "This Part does not apply to -- (a) a commercial or government client; or (b) a third party payer who
-- would be [one] ... but this section and sections 181(1), (7) and (8), 182, 183 and 185(3), (4) and (5) do apply".
-- s 170(2): "a commercial or government client is a client of a law practice where the client is -- (a) a law practice; or (b)
-- [under the Corporations Act] (i) a public company, a subsidiary of a public company, a large proprietary company, a foreign
-- company, a subsidiary of a foreign company or a registered Australian body; (ii) a liquidator, administrator or receiver; (iii)
-- a financial services licensee; ... (d) a partnership that carries on the business of providing professional services if the
-- partnership consists of more than 20 members or if the partnership would be a large proprietary company ...; (e) a body or
-- person incorporated in a place outside Australia; or (f) a person who has agreed to the payment of costs on a basis that is
-- the result of a tender process; or (g) a government authority in Australia or in a foreign country; or (h) a person specified
-- in, or of a class specified in, the Uniform Rules." [General Rules] r 71: Victorian state owned enterprises and New South Wales
-- state owned corporations.
DECLARE `Kind of client` IS ONE OF
    `an individual`
    `a law practice`                                                       -- s 170(2)(a)
    `a public company, a large proprietary company, a foreign company, or a subsidiary of one` -- (b)(i)
    `a liquidator, administrator or receiver`                              -- (b)(ii)
    `a financial services licensee`                                        -- (b)(iii)
    `a professional services partnership of more than 20 members`          -- (d)
    `a body incorporated outside Australia`                                -- (e)
    `a client whose costs basis came from a tender process`                -- (f)
    `a government authority`                                               -- (g)
    `a Victorian state owned enterprise or NSW state owned corporation`    -- (h), General Rules r 71
    `a small proprietary company or other client`

@export Is my client a commercial or government client, so that most of Uniform Law Part 4.3 does not protect it (s 170, General Rules r 71)?
GIVEN `the client` IS A `Kind of client` @desc What kind of client it is
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my client a commercial or government client` IF
    CONSIDER `the client`
    WHEN `an individual` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a small proprietary company or other client` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `s 179 -- the client's right to a costs agreement`

-- [Uniform Law] s 179: "A client of a law practice has the right to require and to have a negotiated costs agreement with the law
-- practice." s 179 is not among the sections s 170(1) keeps for commercial or government clients.
@export Can my client require a negotiated costs agreement with my firm (Uniform Law ss 179, 170)?
GIVEN `the client` IS A `Kind of client` @desc What kind of client it is
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can my client require a costs agreement` IF
    NOT `Is my client a commercial or government client` `the client`

§§ `s 174, Sch 4 cl 18(3), (4), General Rules r 72 -- what I must disclose about costs`

-- [Uniform Law] s 174(1): the practice "(a) must, when or as soon as practicable after instructions are initially given in a
-- matter, provide the client with information disclosing the basis on which legal costs will be calculated in the matter and an
-- estimate of the total legal costs; and (b) must, when or as soon as practicable after there is any significant change to
-- anything previously disclosed ..., provide the client with information disclosing the change". s 174(2)(a): the first
-- disclosure "must include information about the client's rights -- (i) to negotiate a costs agreement ...; (ii) to negotiate the
-- billing method ...; (iii) to receive a bill ... and to request an itemised bill ...; and (iv) to seek the assistance of the
-- designated local regulatory authority in the event of a dispute about legal costs". s 174(3): the practice "must take all
-- reasonable steps to satisfy itself that the client has understood and given consent". s 174(6): "A disclosure under this
-- section must be made in writing".
-- s 174(4): no disclosure is required "if the total legal costs in the matter (excluding GST and disbursements) are not likely
-- to exceed ... (the lower threshold)", though the practice "may nevertheless choose to provide ... the uniform standard disclosure
-- form". s 174(5): below "the higher threshold" the practice "may, instead of making a disclosure under subsection (1), ...
-- [provide] the uniform standard disclosure form".
-- Sch 4 cl 18(3): "Until a Uniform Rule prescribing an amount for the purposes of section 174(4) ... takes effect, the lower
-- threshold is $750". cl 18(4): "... the higher threshold is $3000". [General Rules] r 72: the form is "Form 1 in Schedule 1" for
-- solicitors and "Form 2" for barristers; its Note confirms the $750 and $3000 amounts. No deposited rule prescribes other amounts.
`the lower threshold` MEANS 750
`the higher threshold` MEANS 3000

DECLARE `Disclosure answer` IS ONE OF
    `none: Part 4.3's disclosure duties do not apply to a commercial or government client (s 170(1))`
    `none required: costs are not likely to exceed the $750 lower threshold, though the standard form may be given (s 174(4))`
    `the uniform standard disclosure form will do (Form 1, or Form 2 for a barrister), or full disclosure: costs are not likely to exceed the $3000 higher threshold (s 174(5), General Rules r 72)`
    `full written disclosure: the basis of calculation, an estimate of the total legal costs and the client's four rights, when or as soon as practicable after instructions (s 174(1), (2), (6))`

@export What costs disclosure must I give my client at the start of the matter (Uniform Law ss 170, 174, Sch 4 cl 18)?
GIVEN `the client` IS A `Kind of client` @desc What kind of client it is
      `likely total legal costs excluding GST and disbursements` IS A NUMBER @desc The total legal costs likely in the matter, in dollars, excluding GST and disbursements
GIVETH A `Disclosure answer`
DECIDE `What costs disclosure must I give` IS
    IF `Is my client a commercial or government client` `the client`
    THEN `none: Part 4.3's disclosure duties do not apply to a commercial or government client (s 170(1))`
    ELSE IF `likely total legal costs excluding GST and disbursements` AT MOST `the lower threshold`
         THEN `none required: costs are not likely to exceed the $750 lower threshold, though the standard form may be given (s 174(4))`
         ELSE IF `likely total legal costs excluding GST and disbursements` AT MOST `the higher threshold`
              THEN `the uniform standard disclosure form will do (Form 1, or Form 2 for a barrister), or full disclosure: costs are not likely to exceed the $3000 higher threshold (s 174(5), General Rules r 72)`
              ELSE `full written disclosure: the basis of calculation, an estimate of the total legal costs and the client's four rights, when or as soon as practicable after instructions (s 174(1), (2), (6))`

DECLARE `My costs disclosure` HAS
    `in writing`                                                     IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 174(6)
    `given when or as soon as practicable after instructions`        IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 174(1)(a)
    `states the basis on which costs will be calculated`             IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 174(1)(a)
    `estimates the total legal costs`                                IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 174(1)(a)
    `states the right to negotiate a costs agreement`                IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 174(2)(a)(i)
    `states the right to negotiate the billing method`               IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 174(2)(a)(ii)
    `states the right to a bill and to ask for an itemised bill`     IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 174(2)(a)(iii)
    `states the right to the Board's help in a costs dispute`        IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 174(2)(a)(iv)
    `I took reasonable steps to see the client understood and consented` IS A BOOLEAN -- s 174(3)

@export Does my full costs disclosure meet Uniform Law s 174(1)(a), (2)(a), (3) and (6)?
GIVEN `the disclosure` IS A `My costs disclosure` @desc What your disclosure contained and when and how it was given
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does my disclosure meet section 174` IF
        `the disclosure`'s `in writing`
    AND `the disclosure`'s `given when or as soon as practicable after instructions`
    AND `the disclosure`'s `states the basis on which costs will be calculated`
    AND `the disclosure`'s `estimates the total legal costs`
    AND `the disclosure`'s `states the right to negotiate a costs agreement`
    AND `the disclosure`'s `states the right to negotiate the billing method`
    AND `the disclosure`'s `states the right to a bill and to ask for an itemised bill`
    AND `the disclosure`'s `states the right to the Board's help in a costs dispute`
    AND `the disclosure`'s `I took reasonable steps to see the client understood and consented`

-- s 174(7): where no disclosure was made because costs were below the lower threshold, the practice must, "when or as soon as
-- practicable after [it] becomes aware (or ought reasonably become aware) that the total legal costs ... are likely to exceed the
-- lower threshold -- (a) inform the client in writing of that expectation; and (b) make the disclosure required by subsection (1)
-- or (if applicable) subsection (5)". s 174(8): the same where the standard form was given and costs will exceed the higher
-- threshold, but the disclosure must then be under subsection (1). s 174(1)(b), (2)(b): any significant change to what was
-- disclosed must be disclosed with "a sufficient and reasonable amount of information about the impact of the change".
DECLARE `Disclosure so far` IS ONE OF
    `none, as costs were below the lower threshold`
    `the uniform standard disclosure form`
    `full disclosure`

DECLARE `Further disclosure answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: tell the client in writing that costs will now exceed $750, and give the standard form or full disclosure (s 174(7))`
    `yes: tell the client in writing that costs will now exceed $3000, and give full disclosure (s 174(8))`
    `yes: disclose the significant change and its effect on costs (s 174(1)(b), (2)(b))`
    `no`

@export Must I give my client further costs disclosure now (Uniform Law s 174(1)(b), (7), (8))?
GIVEN `what I have disclosed so far` IS A `Disclosure so far` @desc What disclosure you have already given
      `likely total legal costs now` IS A NUMBER @desc The total legal costs now likely, in dollars, excluding GST and disbursements
      `a significant change to anything disclosed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether anything you disclosed has significantly changed
GIVETH A `Further disclosure answer`
DECIDE `Must I give further disclosure` IS
    IF `what I have disclosed so far` EQUALS `none, as costs were below the lower threshold` AND `likely total legal costs now` GREATER THAN `the lower threshold`
    THEN `yes: tell the client in writing that costs will now exceed $750, and give the standard form or full disclosure (s 174(7))`
    ELSE IF `what I have disclosed so far` EQUALS `the uniform standard disclosure form` AND `likely total legal costs now` GREATER THAN `the higher threshold`
         THEN `yes: tell the client in writing that costs will now exceed $3000, and give full disclosure (s 174(8))`
         ELSE IF `a significant change to anything disclosed`
              THEN `yes: disclose the significant change and its effect on costs (s 174(1)(b), (2)(b))`
              ELSE `no`

§§ `s 177 -- disclosure before a settlement`

-- [Uniform Law] s 177(1): "If a law practice negotiates the settlement of a litigious matter on behalf of a client, the law practice
-- must disclose to the client, before the settlement is executed -- (a) a reasonable estimate of the amount of legal costs payable by
-- the client if the matter is settled (including any legal costs of another party that the client is to pay); and (b) a reasonable
-- estimate of any contributions towards those costs likely to be received from another party." s 177(2): a practice retained by
-- another practice need not, "if the other law practice makes the disclosure". Whether an arbitration is a "litigious matter" is
-- the fork recorded in the Application Act baseline (lpula-costs.l4, "Is my matter a litigious matter"); here it is a fact.
@export Must I give my client estimates of the costs payable and contributions expected before a settlement is executed (Uniform Law s 177)?
GIVEN `the client` IS A `Kind of client` @desc What kind of client it is
      `it is a litigious matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the matter involves, or is reasonably likely to involve, proceedings in a court or tribunal
      `my firm negotiates the settlement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your firm negotiates the settlement for the client
      `another firm that retained us makes the disclosure` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a firm that retained yours makes the disclosure before settlement
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I disclose costs before settlement` IF
        (NOT `Is my client a commercial or government client` `the client`)
    AND `it is a litigious matter`
    AND `my firm negotiates the settlement`
    AND (NOT `another firm that retained us makes the disclosure`)

§§ `ss 172(4), 178, General Rules r 72A -- if disclosure was not made`

-- [Uniform Law] s 178(1): "If a law practice contravenes the disclosure obligations of this Part -- (a) the costs agreement
-- concerned (if any) is void; and (b) the client ... is not required to pay the legal costs until they have been assessed or any
-- costs dispute has been determined by the designated local regulatory authority; and (c) the law practice must not commence or
-- maintain proceedings for the recovery of any or all of the legal costs until they have been assessed or any costs dispute has
-- been determined ...; and (d) the contravention is capable of constituting unsatisfactory professional conduct or professional
-- misconduct on the part of any principal of the law practice or any legal practitioner associate ... involved".
-- [General Rules] r 72A(2): s 178(1) and (2) "do not apply ... in circumstances where the relevant authority, a costs assessor, a
-- court or a tribunal is satisfied that -- (a) the law practice took reasonable steps to comply ... before becoming aware of the
-- contravention, and (b) the law practice, no later than 14 days after the date on which it became aware of the contravention,
-- rectified the contravention, as far as practicable, ..., and (c) the contravention was not substantial and it would not be
-- reasonable to expect that the client would have made a different decision". That satisfaction is a judgment for the authority,
-- assessor, court or tribunal: the answer is conditional on it.
DECLARE `Non-disclosure answer` IS ONE OF
    `the costs agreement is void, the client need not pay until the costs are assessed or the dispute determined, my firm may not sue until then, and it may be a conduct matter against those involved (s 178(1))`
    `s 178(1) does not apply: the decision-maker is satisfied of reasonable steps, rectification within 14 days, and no substantial effect (General Rules r 72A)`
    `nothing: the disclosure duties were met`

@export What follows if my firm did not make the costs disclosure Part 4.3 requires (Uniform Law s 178, General Rules r 72A)?
GIVEN `my firm contravened the disclosure obligations` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your firm failed to make a disclosure Part 4.3 requires
      `the decision-maker is satisfied of the r 72A matters` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Board, a costs assessor, a court or a tribunal is satisfied that your firm took reasonable steps, rectified within 14 days, and the contravention was not substantial
GIVETH A `Non-disclosure answer`
DECIDE `What follows from not disclosing` IS
    IF (NOT `my firm contravened the disclosure obligations`)
    THEN `nothing: the disclosure duties were met`
    ELSE IF `the decision-maker is satisfied of the r 72A matters`
         THEN `s 178(1) does not apply: the decision-maker is satisfied of reasonable steps, rectification within 14 days, and no substantial effect (General Rules r 72A)`
         ELSE `the costs agreement is void, the client need not pay until the costs are assessed or the dispute determined, my firm may not sue until then, and it may be a conduct matter against those involved (s 178(1))`

-- [Uniform Law] s 172(4): "A costs agreement is prima facie evidence that legal costs disclosed in the agreement are fair and
-- reasonable if -- (a) the provisions of Division 3 relating to costs disclosure have been complied with; and (b) the costs
-- agreement does not contravene, and was not entered into in contravention of, any provision of Division 4."
@export Is my costs agreement prima facie evidence that the costs it discloses are fair and reasonable (Uniform Law s 172(4))?
GIVEN `the disclosure duties were complied with` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether Part 4.3 Division 3 (costs disclosure) was complied with
      `the agreement complies with Division 4` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the costs agreement neither contravenes nor was made in contravention of Part 4.3 Division 4
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my costs agreement prima facie evidence of fair costs` IF
    `the disclosure duties were complied with` AND `the agreement complies with Division 4`

§§ `ss 187, 190, 191 -- bills and reports`

-- [Uniform Law] s 187(1): if a bill is "in the form of a lump sum bill, any person who is entitled to apply for an assessment of
-- the legal costs ... may request the law practice to give the person an itemised bill." (2): "A request for an itemised bill must
-- be made within 30 days after the date on which the legal costs become payable." (3): "The law practice must comply with the
-- request within 21 days after the date on which the request is made".
@export Can my client still ask for an itemised bill (Uniform Law s 187(1), (2))?
GIVEN `the bill was a lump sum bill` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your firm's bill was a lump sum bill
      `the date the costs became payable` IS A DATE @desc The date the legal costs became payable
      `the date of the request` IS A DATE @desc The date the client asks for an itemised bill
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can my client still ask for an itemised bill` IF
        `the bill was a lump sum bill`
    AND Day `the date of the request` AT MOST Day `the date the costs became payable` PLUS 30

@export Is my firm's itemised bill in time (Uniform Law s 187(3): within 21 days of the request)?
GIVEN `the date of the request` IS A DATE @desc The date the client asked for an itemised bill
      `the date we gave it` IS A DATE @desc The date your firm gives the itemised bill
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is our itemised bill in time` IF
    Day `the date we gave it` AT MOST Day `the date of the request` PLUS 21

-- s 191: "A law practice must not make a charge for preparing or giving a bill, and any charge made for that purpose is not
-- recoverable by the law practice."
@export How much may my firm charge the client for preparing or giving a bill (Uniform Law s 191), in dollars?
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How much may my firm charge for a bill` IS 0

-- s 190(1): the practice "must give a client, on reasonable request, without charge and within a reasonable period, a written
-- report of the legal costs incurred by the client to date, or since the last bill". (2): a practice retained by another practice
-- reports to that practice instead, (3) unless that practice "ceases to act for the client in the matter when the law practice is
-- retained".
@export Must my firm give the client a free written report of costs to date (Uniform Law s 190)?
GIVEN `the client` IS A `Kind of client` @desc What kind of client it is
      `the client made a reasonable request` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the client has reasonably asked for a report
      `another firm retained us for the client` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether another firm retained yours on the client's behalf
      `that firm stopped acting when it retained us` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that firm ceased to act for the client when it retained yours
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must my firm give a costs progress report` IF
        (NOT `Is my client a commercial or government client` `the client`)
    AND `the client made a reasonable request`
    AND ((NOT `another firm retained us for the client`) OR `that firm stopped acting when it retained us`)

§§ `ss 178(1)(c), 194, 198(7)(b) -- may my firm sue for its costs`

-- [Uniform Law] s 194(1): the practice "must not commence legal proceedings to recover legal costs from a person unless a bill has
-- been given for the legal costs and the bill complies with the requirements of this Law and the Uniform Rules." (2): nor "until --
-- (a) where the legal costs are the subject of a costs dispute before the designated local regulatory authority -- the authority
-- has closed or resolved the dispute; and (b) at least 30 days after the later of -- (i) the date on which the person is given the
-- bill; or (ii) the date on which the person receives an itemised bill following a request made in accordance with section 187."
-- s 198(7)(b): once a costs assessment is applied for, "the law practice must not commence any proceedings to recover the legal
-- costs until the costs assessment has been completed". s 178(1)(c): after a disclosure failure, not "until they have been
-- assessed or any costs dispute has been determined".
DECLARE `My unpaid bill` HAS
    `a bill complying with the Law and the Rules was given`          IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 194(1)
    `the date the bill was given`                                    IS A DATE     -- s 194(2)(b)(i)
    `the client received an itemised bill after asking under s 187`  IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 194(2)(b)(ii)
    `the date the itemised bill was received`                        IS A DATE
    `a costs dispute before the Board is unresolved`                 IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 194(2)(a)
    `a costs assessment is applied for and not complete`             IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 198(7)(b)
    `disclosure failed and the costs are not yet assessed or determined` IS A BOOLEAN -- s 178(1)(c)

DECLARE `Suing answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: my firm may now commence proceedings`
    `no: no bill complying with the Law and the Rules has been given (s 194(1))`
    `no: disclosure was not made, so the costs must first be assessed or the dispute determined (s 178(1)(c))`
    `no: a costs assessment has been applied for and is not complete (s 198(7)(b))`
    `no: the costs dispute before the Board has not been closed or resolved (s 194(2)(a))`
    `not yet: 30 days have not passed since the bill, or the itemised bill (s 194(2)(b))`

@export May my firm sue the client for its unpaid legal costs now (Uniform Law ss 178(1)(c), 194, 198(7)(b))?
GIVEN `the bill` IS A `My unpaid bill` @desc The bill and what has happened since
      `today` IS A DATE @desc The date your firm would commence proceedings
GIVETH A `Suing answer`
DECIDE `May my firm sue for its costs` IS
    IF (NOT `the bill`'s `a bill complying with the Law and the Rules was given`)
    THEN `no: no bill complying with the Law and the Rules has been given (s 194(1))`
    ELSE IF `the bill`'s `disclosure failed and the costs are not yet assessed or determined`
         THEN `no: disclosure was not made, so the costs must first be assessed or the dispute determined (s 178(1)(c))`
         ELSE IF `the bill`'s `a costs assessment is applied for and not complete`
              THEN `no: a costs assessment has been applied for and is not complete (s 198(7)(b))`
              ELSE IF `the bill`'s `a costs dispute before the Board is unresolved`
                   THEN `no: the costs dispute before the Board has not been closed or resolved (s 194(2)(a))`
                   ELSE IF Day `today` LESS THAN `the later date` PLUS 30
                        THEN `not yet: 30 days have not passed since the bill, or the itemised bill (s 194(2)(b))`
                        ELSE `yes: my firm may now commence proceedings`
    WHERE
        `the later date` MEANS
            IF     `the bill`'s `the client received an itemised bill after asking under s 187`
               AND Day (`the bill`'s `the date the itemised bill was received`) GREATER THAN Day (`the bill`'s `the date the bill was given`)
            THEN Day (`the bill`'s `the date the itemised bill was received`)
            ELSE Day (`the bill`'s `the date the bill was given`)

§§ `s 195, General Rules r 75 -- interest on unpaid costs`

-- [Uniform Law] s 195(1): interest may be charged "in accordance with the applicable terms of a costs agreement". (2): otherwise
-- "if the costs are unpaid 30 days or more after the law practice has given a bill". (3): not "unless the bill for those costs
-- contains a statement that interest is payable and of the rate of interest". (4): not "at a rate that exceeds the rate specified
-- in or determined under the Uniform Rules". (5): not "on a bill given more than 6 months after the completion of the matter",
-- (6) unless a lump sum bill was given within the 6 months and the itemised bill was requested after them, or the bill was not
-- issued within the 6 months "at the request of the client".
-- [General Rules] r 75(1): "the rate of interest is the rate that is equal to the Cash Rate Target as at the relevant date,
-- increased by 2 percentage points"; r 75(2): "relevant date means the date the bill was issued".
@export What is the highest rate of interest my firm may charge on unpaid legal costs (Uniform Law s 195(4), General Rules r 75), in percent a year?
GIVEN `the Cash Rate Target when the bill was issued` IS A NUMBER @desc The Reserve Bank's Cash Rate Target, in percent, on the date the bill was issued
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the highest interest rate my firm may charge` IS
    `the Cash Rate Target when the bill was issued` PLUS 2

DECLARE `My interest claim` HAS
    `a costs agreement provides for interest`                        IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 195(1)
    `days the costs have been unpaid since the bill`                 IS A NUMBER   -- s 195(2)
    `the bill states that interest is payable and the rate`          IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 195(3)
    `the rate charged`                                               IS A NUMBER   -- s 195(4)
    `the Cash Rate Target when the bill was issued`                  IS A NUMBER   -- r 75
    `the bill was given more than 6 months after the matter was completed` IS A BOOLEAN -- s 195(5)
    `the lateness came from the client's request or a late itemised bill request` IS A BOOLEAN -- s 195(6)

@export May my firm charge this interest on the unpaid legal costs (Uniform Law s 195, General Rules r 75)?
GIVEN `the claim` IS A `My interest claim` @desc The bill, the costs agreement and the interest charged
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May my firm charge this interest` IF
        (`the claim`'s `a costs agreement provides for interest` OR `the claim`'s `days the costs have been unpaid since the bill` AT LEAST 30)
    AND `the claim`'s `the bill states that interest is payable and the rate`
    AND `the claim`'s `the rate charged` AT MOST `What is the highest interest rate my firm may charge` (`the claim`'s `the Cash Rate Target when the bill was issued`)
    AND (   (NOT `the claim`'s `the bill was given more than 6 months after the matter was completed`)
         OR `the claim`'s `the lateness came from the client's request or a late itemised bill request`)

§§ `ss 197, 198 -- may my client have my costs assessed`

-- [Uniform Law] s 198(1): "Applications for an assessment of the whole or any part of legal costs payable to a law practice may be
-- made by ... (a) a client who has paid or is liable to pay them". (3): "within 12 months after -- (a) the bill was given to, or the
-- request for payment was made to, the client ...; or (b) the legal costs were paid if neither a bill nor a request was made." (4):
-- a late application "may be dealt with by the costs assessor if the designated tribunal ... determines, after having regard to the
-- delay and the reasons for the delay, that it is just and fair". s 197: costs that are the subject of a costs dispute under
-- Chapter 5 may be assessed only if the authority "is unable to resolve the costs dispute and has notified the parties of their
-- entitlement to apply", or arranges the assessment itself.
-- s 170(1) keeps none of Division 7 for a commercial or government client. Read literally, such a client cannot apply under
-- s 198; but s 198(5) then speaks of third party payers whom s 170(1)(b) has already excluded (a tension, recorded in NOTES).
-- The anniversary itself is taken to be "within 12 months" (an assumption of this encoding).
DECLARE `Assessment answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the application is within 12 months (s 198(3))`
    `yes, though late: the designated tribunal has found it just and fair to deal with it (s 198(4))`
    `out of time: it can be dealt with only if the designated tribunal finds it just and fair (s 198(4))`
    `not yet: the costs are in a costs dispute the Board has not referred to assessment (s 197)`
    `not under Part 4.3: it does not apply to a commercial or government client (s 170(1))`

@export Can my client apply to have my firm's legal costs assessed (Uniform Law ss 170, 197, 198)?
GIVEN `the client` IS A `Kind of client` @desc What kind of client it is
      `the date of the bill, request for payment or payment` IS A DATE @desc The date the bill or request for payment was given, or, if neither, the date the costs were paid
      `the date of the application` IS A DATE @desc The date the client applies for a costs assessment
      `a costs dispute is with the Board and not referred` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the costs are in a costs dispute before the Board that it has neither declared unresolvable nor sent to assessment
      `the designated tribunal has found a late application just and fair` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the designated tribunal has determined that it is just and fair to deal with a late application
GIVETH AN `Assessment answer`
DECIDE `Can my client have my costs assessed` IS
    IF `Is my client a commercial or government client` `the client`
    THEN `not under Part 4.3: it does not apply to a commercial or government client (s 170(1))`
    ELSE IF `a costs dispute is with the Board and not referred`
         THEN `not yet: the costs are in a costs dispute the Board has not referred to assessment (s 197)`
         ELSE IF Day `the date of the application` AT MOST Day (`months after` `the date of the bill, request for payment or payment` 12)
              THEN `yes: the application is within 12 months (s 198(3))`
              ELSE IF `the designated tribunal has found a late application just and fair`
                   THEN `yes, though late: the designated tribunal has found it just and fair to deal with it (s 198(4))`
                   ELSE `out of time: it can be dealt with only if the designated tribunal finds it just and fair (s 198(4))`

§§ `s 204 -- who pays for the assessment`

-- [Uniform Law] s 204(2): "Unless the costs assessor believes that in all the circumstances it is not fair and reasonable for the
-- costs to be paid otherwise, the costs of a costs assessment are payable by a law practice if -- (a) the law practice has failed to
-- disclose a matter required to be disclosed by Division 3; or (b) ... in the manner required by Division 3; or (c) the law
-- practice's costs have been reduced by 15% or more on assessment." Otherwise s 204(1): the assessor determines who pays.
DECLARE `Assessment costs answer` IS ONE OF
    `my firm pays them (s 204(2))`
    `as the costs assessor determines (s 204(1))`

@export Who pays the costs of the costs assessment (Uniform Law s 204)?
GIVEN `my firm failed to disclose as Division 3 requires` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your firm failed to disclose a matter, or to disclose it in the required manner, under Part 4.3 Division 3
      `the amount billed` IS A NUMBER @desc The legal costs your firm billed, in dollars
      `the amount assessed` IS A NUMBER @desc The legal costs as assessed, in dollars
      `the assessor finds it unfair for my firm to pay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the costs assessor believes it not fair and reasonable in all the circumstances for your firm to pay
GIVETH AN `Assessment costs answer`
DECIDE `Who pays for the costs assessment` IS
    IF     (   `my firm failed to disclose as Division 3 requires`
            OR (`the amount billed` MINUS `the amount assessed`) TIMES 100 AT LEAST `the amount billed` TIMES 15)
       AND (NOT `the assessor finds it unfair for my firm to pay`)
    THEN `my firm pays them (s 204(2))`
    ELSE `as the costs assessor determines (s 204(1))`

§§ `ss 188, 207 -- can an unfair bill be held against me`

-- [Uniform Law] s 188(1): a bill or its covering letter must be signed by, or nominate, "a principal ... as the responsible
-- principal for the bill". (2): if none is, "each principal of the law practice is taken to be a responsible principal".
-- s 207(1): charging more than fair and reasonable costs "is capable of constituting unsatisfactory professional conduct or
-- professional misconduct on the part of -- (a) the responsible principal or principals for a bill ...; and (b) each legal
-- practitioner associate ... who was involved in giving the bill or authorising it to be given." (2): for a responsible principal,
-- "whether or not he or she had actual knowledge". (3): it "does not apply to a responsible principal if he or she establishes that
-- it was not reasonable for him or her to suspect or believe that the legal costs in the bill were unfair or unreasonable". The s
-- 207(3) defence is for a responsible principal only (and, taking "subsection (1) does not apply", covers both limbs for him);
-- an associate who is not a responsible principal but was involved in giving the bill has none.
@export Can my firm's charging of more than fair and reasonable costs be a conduct matter against me (Uniform Law ss 188, 207)?
GIVEN `the costs were more than fair and reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the bill charged more than fair and reasonable legal costs
      `I signed the bill or was nominated as responsible principal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you signed the bill as, or were nominated as, its responsible principal
      `I am a principal and no principal signed or was nominated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a principal of the firm and no principal signed or was nominated for the bill
      `I was involved in giving or authorising the bill` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were involved in giving the bill or authorising it to be given
      `I can show I had no reason to suspect the costs were unfair` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can establish it was not reasonable for you to suspect or believe the costs were unfair or unreasonable
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can an unfair bill be a conduct matter against me` IF
        `the costs were more than fair and reasonable`
    AND IF `I signed the bill or was nominated as responsible principal` OR `I am a principal and no principal signed or was nominated`
        THEN NOT `I can show I had no reason to suspect the costs were unfair`
        ELSE `I was involved in giving or authorising the bill`
